{"abstract":"In living beings, the natural development of the body has been shown to facilitate learning. The application of these natural developmental principles in robotics have been considered in different robotic morphologies and scenarios, leading to mixed results. Development was found to be beneficial for learning in some instances, but also irrelevant or detrimental in others. This mix of results and scenarios has allowed researchers to extract some notions about the conditions that must be fulfilled or set to apply morphological development successfully. Notions that we have organized to set a series of design conditions to successfully apply morphological development. Thus, in this article, we are going to focus on the study of one of them that has been frequently addressed by researchers in their studies in very general terms. It can be described as the need to achieve a suitable synergy among the different components involved in the development and learning process: morphological development strategy, controller, task, and learning algorithm. In particular, we have concentrated on empirically determining the influence of five developmental strategies, implemented in different ways, applied at different speeds and deployed in different orders and combinations, over the problem of a NAO robot controlled by an artificial neural network obtained through a neuroevolutionary algorithm learning a bipedal walking task. The results obtained permit providing a more detailed description of what a suitable synergy implies and how it can be utilized to design more successful morphological developmental processes to improve robot learning.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Naya, Martin","Faina, Andres","Duro, Richard J."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382494"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Spain"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Universidade da Coruña","University of A Coruna"],"keyword":["Bipedal walking","Engineering design","Morphological Development","Robot learning"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"engineering morphological development in a robotic bipedal walking problem an empirical study","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neucom.2023.01.003","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382494","identifier":[{"id":"85146428620","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-51ebdf06-2954-4067-9216-aa92aba0a28d"],"keyword":["Bipedal walking","Engineering design","Morphological development","Robot learning"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-3973B2CA0A7614BD72439B5C9D2C233B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/92897483/1_s2.0_S0925231223000115_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neucom.2023.01.003","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"es","key":"university of a coruna","loc":["Spain","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of A Coruna","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"es","key":"universidade da coruña","loc":["Spain","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Universidade da Coruña","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Martin","key":"nayapau","last":"Naya","name":"Naya, Martin","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Richard J.","key":"duropau","last":"Duro","name":"Duro, Richard J.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.neucom.2023.01.003","issn":["09252312","18728286"],"issn_org":["09252312"],"journal":"Neurocomputing","pages":"83-99","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Neurocomputing"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85146428620"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-51ebdf06-2954-4067-9216-aa92aba0a28d"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Engineering Morphological Development in a Robotic Bipedal Walking Problem: An Empirical Study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382494","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"In human beings, the joint development of the body and cognitive system has been shown to facilitate the acquisition of new skills and abilities. In the literature, these natural principles have been applied to robotics with mixed results and different authors have suggested several hypotheses to explain them. One of the most popular hypotheses states that morphological development improves learning by increasing exploration of the solution space, avoiding stagnation in local optima. In this article, we are going to study the influence of growth-based morphological development and its nuances as a tool to improve the exploration of the solution space. We will perform a series of experiments over two different robot morphologies which learn to walk. Furthermore, we will compare these results to another optimization strategy that has been shown to be useful to favor exploration in learning algorithms: the application of noise during learning. Finally, to check if the increased exploration hypothesis holds, we visualize the genotypic space during learning considering the different optimization strategies by using the Search Trajectory Network representation. The results indicate that noise and growth increase exploration, but only growth guides the search towards good solutions.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Naya, Martin","Faina, Andres","Duro, Richard"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-712556"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Spain"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Universidade da Coruña","University of A Coruna"],"keyword":["Cognitive development","Exploration in Learning","Morphological Development","Optimization Strategies","Robotics"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"guiding the exploration of the solution space in walking robots through growth based morphological development","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/102421888/2023_GECCO_Lisbon_v09.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-712556","identifier":[{"id":"85167693086","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-d006b050-2e91-4ba4-904f-d6aa62bb90a4"],"keyword":["Cognitive Development","Exploration in Learning","Morphological Development","Optimization Strategies","Robotics"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-E9C18B106382755B216845440A0D8255"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/102421888/2023_GECCO_Lisbon_v09.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"es","key":"university of a coruna","loc":["Spain","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of A Coruna","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"es","key":"universidade da coruña","loc":["Spain","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Universidade da Coruña","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Martin","key":"nayapau","last":"Naya","name":"Naya, Martin","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Richard","key":"duropau","last":"Duro","name":"Duro, Richard","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3583131.3590489","pages":"1230-1238","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"GECCO '23: Proceedings of the Genetic and Evolutionary Computation Conference"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85167693086"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-d006b050-2e91-4ba4-904f-d6aa62bb90a4"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Guiding the Exploration of the Solution Space in Walking Robots Through Growth-Based Morphological Development","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-712556","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"With the recent advances of modular robots and low-cost manipulators, the evolution of robots, including morphologies and controllers, has become possible to perform in a physical setup without using any simulators. In this scenario, the evolution cannot be parallelized and the wall time becomes a scarce resource that should be used wisely. This paper analyses different algorithms by using the wall time as a stopping criterion for evolution, and it takes into account that wall time depends on the evaluation time plus the time to assemble and disassemble robots before and after an evaluation. The experiments have been performed in simulation, but the evaluation and assembly time have been carefully modelled from previous hardware experiments. Results suggest that (i) genetic algorithms are severely penalized, (ii) genetic algorithms can be improved by performing several evaluations of controllers for each morphology, and that (iii) evolutionary strategies that can chain several evaluations of robots with close morphologies can outperform other evolutionary algorithms. This finding is not surprising, but to the best of our knowledge previous attempts to evolve modular robots in hardware have not employed evolutionary strategies.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Moreno, Rodrigo","Faina, Andres"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382300"],"event":{"acronym":"EvoApplications","title":"European Conference on the Applications of Evolutionary Computation"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Evolution in hardware","Evolutionary Robotics","Modular Robots","Morphological evolution"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"out of time on the constrains that evolution in hardware faces when evolving modular robots","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/90792581/Moreno.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382300","identifier":[{"id":"85129325154","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-45896aaf-ddc7-43df-befa-710f004bd4fd"],"keyword":["Evolution in hardware","Evolutionary robotics","Modular robots","Morphological evolution"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-EDBD45FB3B1BD0BABFF14AD971E21266"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/90792581/Moreno.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rodrigo","key":"morenopau","last":"Moreno","locid":"5c54c44a-9eaa-4a97-a78a-e853dc73e9d9","name":"Moreno, Rodrigo","orcid":"0000-0003-3738-010X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Juan Luis","key":"jimenezlaredopedh","last":"Jimenez Laredo","name":"Jimenez Laredo, Juan Luis","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"J. Ignacio","key":"hidalgopedh","last":"Hidalgo","name":"Hidalgo, J. Ignacio","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Kehinde","key":"oluwatoyinbabaagbapedh","last":"Oluwatoyin Babaagba","name":"Oluwatoyin Babaagba, Kehinde","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-031-02462-7_42","eisbn":["9783031024627"],"isbn":["9783031024610"],"issn":["03029743"],"publisher":"Springer","series":"Lecture notes in computer science","status":"p","subtitle":"25th European Conference, EvoApplications 2022, Held as Part of EvoStar 2022, Madrid, Spain, April 20–22, 2022, Proceedings","title":"Applications of Evolutionary Computation"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85129325154"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-45896aaf-ddc7-43df-befa-710f004bd4fd"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Out of Time: On the Constrains that Evolution in Hardware Faces When Evolving Modular Robots","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382300","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"The recent pandemic has forced us to teach online, which is especially difficult for hand-on courses in robotics, like basic electronics. In this paper, I present a simulator which tries to replicate the same experience students will encounter during the exercises in the laboratory. The simulator has been developed in Fritzing, uses realistic multimeters for measuring and checks for common mistakes. Results show not only that the simulator was extremely useful during the pandemic, but also that it can supplement laboratory exercises when teaching in-classroom.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Faina, Andres"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381287"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Fritzing simulator","Online teaching","Robotics Education","Simulation Tools","pandemic education adaptations"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"learning hands on electronics from home a simulator for fritzing","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/90459472/2022CLAWAR_Fritzing_Simulator_postprint.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381287","ids":["itu-024563e9-8f51-4a5e-8055-569b4061546f"],"keyword":["Fritzing simulator","online teaching","pandemic education adaptations","robotics education","simulation tools"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-749A71C592AB7CC7479198163C4ADF95"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/90459472/2022CLAWAR_Fritzing_Simulator_postprint.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"eisbn":["23673389"],"isbn":["23673370"],"issue":"530","series":"Lectiure Notes in Networks and Systems","status":"p","title":"Robotics in Natural Settings: CLAWAR 2022"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-024563e9-8f51-4a5e-8055-569b4061546f"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Learning Hands-On Electronics from Home: A Simulator for Fritzing","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381287","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"In nature, the physical development of the body that takes place in parallel to the cognitive development of the individual has been shown to facilitate learning. This opens up the question of whether the same principles could be applied to robots in order to accelerate the learning of controllers and, if so, how to apply them effectively. In this line, several authors have run experiments, usually quite complex and heterogeneous, with different levels of success. In some cases, morphological development seemed to provide an advantage and in others it was clearly irrelevant or even detrimental. Basically, morphological development seems to provide an advantage only under some specific conditions, which cannot be identified before running an experiment. This is due the fact that there is still no agreement on the underlying mechanisms that lead to success or on how to design morphological development processes for specific problems. In this paper, we address this issue through the execution of different experiments over a simple, replicable, and straightforward experimental setup that makes use of different neural network controlled walkers together with a morphological development strategy based on growth. The morphological development processes in these experiments are analyzed both in terms of the results obtained by the different walkers and in terms of how their fitness landscapes change as the morphologies develop. By comparing experiments where morphological development improves learning and where it does not, a series of initial insights have been extracted on how to design morphological development processes.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Naya, Martin","Faina, Andres","Mallo, Alma","Duro, Richard"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381686"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Spain"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Universidade da Coruña","University of A Coruna"],"keyword":["Fitness Landscape","Legged locomotion","Morphological Development","Robot learning"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a study of growth based morphological development in neural network controlled walkers","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neucom.2021.09.082","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381686","identifier":[{"id":"85132386436","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-1bef4c99-eedb-49dd-a52b-5146bd96112d"],"keyword":["Fitness landscape","Legged locomotion","Morphological development","Robot learning"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-21CDB9BCF66B6F999AE2CF67F820BB72"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neucom.2021.09.082","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"rem","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0925231222006385/pdfft?md5=246184e0124b1e7408837fcb110e3d49&pid=1-s2.0-S0925231222006385-main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/102674445/1-s2.0-S0925231222006385-main.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"es","key":"university of a coruna","loc":["Spain","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of A Coruna","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"es","key":"universidade da coruña","loc":["Spain","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Universidade da Coruña","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Martin","key":"nayapau","last":"Naya","name":"Naya, Martin","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Alma","key":"mallopau","last":"Mallo","name":"Mallo, Alma","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Richard","key":"duropau","last":"Duro","name":"Duro, Richard","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.neucom.2021.09.082","issn":["09252312","18728286"],"issn_org":["09252312"],"journal":"Neurocomputing","pages":"279-294","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Neurocomputing","volume":"500"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85132386436"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-1bef4c99-eedb-49dd-a52b-5146bd96112d"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"A Study of Growth Based Morphological Development in Neural Network Controlled Walkers","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381686","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"This work presents a novel sensor setup for the general detection of liquid-liquid interfaces in different mixes of liquids as part of a liquid-liquid extraction device. The sensor setup is applied to a laboratory scale separatory funnel. It uses a near infrared sensor array which receives light going through the liquids inside the funnel, which are illuminated by a light source located on the other side and below the funnel. Light refracts inside the funnel and the liquids and reflects on the interface creating changing patterns in the light intensity measured by the sensor, providing a way of locating the liquid-liquid interface. Liquid mixes with different optical features, from transparent to opaque, emulsion and clean, are used to test whether different types of interfaces produce a distinguishable response on the sensor, allowing to detect interfaces in different situations that can occur as part of an Artificial Intelligence orchestrated battery chemical synthesis process. Emulsion interfaces create a discernible change in the sensor input by lowering the light intensity registered when crossing in front of the sensor making them easier to locate than with other optical techniques. The setup opens the possibility of detecting a liquid-liquid interface as it is forming and can be miniaturized to be attached to laboratory funnels as a manual aid or used with other transparent vessels in automatic solutions like liquid handling robots or pipetting robots.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Moreno, Rodrigo","Faina, Andres","Støy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381821"],"filters":{"added":"2022-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Emulsion","Liquid Liquid Extraction","NIR sensor","general LLE","liquid liquid interface"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"near infrared sensor setup for general interface detection in automatic liquid liquid extraction processes","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/JSEN.2022.3164188","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381821","identifier":[{"id":"85127488530","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-24f06edd-5cc5-44e1-87c9-d1b89216b13e"],"keyword":["NIR sensor","emulsion","general LLE","liquid-liquid extraction","liquid-liquid interface"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-603303DE8495ED8ADD8B6D2D27C99323"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/JSEN.2022.3164188","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"rem","url":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/stamp/stamp.jsp?tp=&arnumber=9745914","version":"Pre-print"},{"access":"oa","license":"Andet","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/101333729/Near_Infrared_Sensor_Setup_for_General_Interface_Detection_in_Automatic_Liquid-Liquid_Extraction_Processes.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rodrigo","key":"morenopau","last":"Moreno","locid":"5c54c44a-9eaa-4a97-a78a-e853dc73e9d9","name":"Moreno, Rodrigo","orcid":"0000-0003-3738-010X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/jsen.2022.3164188","issn":["1530437X","15581748"],"issn_org":["15581748"],"issue":"10","journal":"IEEE Sensors Journal","pages":"9857-9867","publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"IEEE Sensors Journal","volume":"22"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85127488530"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-24f06edd-5cc5-44e1-87c9-d1b89216b13e"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Near Infrared Sensor Setup for General Interface Detection in Automatic Liquid-Liquid Extraction Processes","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381821","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"In human beings, the natural development of the body has been shown to facilitate learning. This approach has been applied in robotic learning with different results, being an advantage under some conditions and tasks. While it is still not well understood under what conditions morphological development helps to learn, several authors have proposed some high-level notions about when it could be interesting to apply it. In our previous work, we have used these notions with the objective of designing a morphological development strategy that facilitates learning in a bipedal locomotion task with an Artificial Neural Network (ANN) controlled robot. In this paper, we aim to go beyond the qualitative design principles previously used and support such considerations with an empirical quantitative study. An analysis of the learning results and how they are related to the design conditions that were established is carried out based on the evolution of the fitness landscape for each developmental stage. The long-term objective is to develop morphology-agnostic optimization strategies for morphological development, which would reduce the number of samples required and, thus, the computational cost, of learning in ANN-controlled robots.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Naya, Martin","Faina, Andres","Duro, Richard"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381709"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Spain"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Universidade da Coruña","University of A Coruna"],"keyword":["Artificial neural networks","Bipedal locomotion","Fitness landscape analysis","Morphological Development","Robotic Learning"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"harnessing growth based morphological development to facilitate learning ann controlled bipedal walking","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/90466834/2022_IJCNN_Padua.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381709","identifier":[{"id":"85140768952","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-1db25f84-0704-4dc7-a40d-3492bc6dd20f"],"keyword":["Artificial Neural Networks","Bipedal locomotion","Fitness landscape analysis","Morphological development","Robotic learning"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-D08C89B979509DA45B51115D14E10FE4"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/90466834/2022_IJCNN_Padua.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"es","key":"university of a coruna","loc":["Spain","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of A Coruna","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"es","key":"universidade da coruña","loc":["Spain","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Universidade da Coruña","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Martin","key":"nayapau","last":"Naya","name":"Naya, Martin","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Richard","key":"duropau","last":"Duro","name":"Duro, Richard","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/ijcnn55064.2022.9892609","isbn":["9781728186719"],"publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the International Joint Conference on Neural Networks (IJCNN)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85140768952"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-1db25f84-0704-4dc7-a40d-3492bc6dd20f"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Harnessing Growth-Based Morphological Development to Facilitate Learning ANN-Controlled Bipedal Walking","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381709","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Materials with the ability to self-classify their own shape have the potential to advance a wide range of engineering applications and industries. Biological systems possess the ability not only to self-reconfigure but also to self-classify themselves to determine a general shape and function. Previous work into modular robotics systems has only enabled self-recognition and self-reconfiguration into a specific target shape, missing the inherent robustness present in nature to self-classify. In this paper we therefore take advantage of recent advances in deep learning and neural cellular automata, and present a simple modular 2D robotic system that can infer its own class of shape through the local communication of its components. Furthermore, we show that our system can be successfully transferred to hardware which thus opens opportunities for future self-classifying machines. Code available at https://github.com/kattwalker/projectcube. Video available at https://youtu.be/0TCOkE4keyc.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Walker, Kathryn Elizabeth","Berg Palm, Rasmus","Moreno, Rodrigo","Faina, Andres","Støy, Kasper","Risi, Sebastian"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381412"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Biomimetics","Deep learning","Modular robotics","Neural Cellular Automata","self classifying systems"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"physical neural cellular automata for 000000000002d shape classification","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/90884499/project_cube_for_IROS_2022_5.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381412","ids":["itu-0a957b67-d70e-453c-8f8b-bd5c0e3311e6"],"keyword":["biomimetics","deep learning","modular robotics","neural cellular automata","self-classifying systems"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-078E5851A4A04C7A48C21E04D5594FE1"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/90884499/project_cube_for_IROS_2022_5.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Play, Culture, and AI, Creative AI Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kathryn Elizabeth","key":"walkerpau","last":"Walker","name":"Walker, Kathryn Elizabeth","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Rasmus","key":"bergpalmpau","last":"Berg Palm","name":"Berg Palm, Rasmus","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rodrigo Moreno","key":"garciapau","last":"Garcia","locid":"5c54c44a-9eaa-4a97-a78a-e853dc73e9d9","name":"Moreno, Rodrigo","name_org":"Garcia, Rodrigo Moreno","orcid":"0000-0003-3738-010X","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sebastian","key":"risipau","last":"Risi","locid":"a420ffb3-2ca6-4925-a25f-baaa5228fc15","name":"Risi, Sebastian","orcid":"0000-0003-3607-8400","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"journal":"IROS","status":"p","title":"IROS","title_org":"IROS 2022"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-0a957b67-d70e-453c-8f8b-bd5c0e3311e6"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Physical Neural Cellular Automata for 2D Shape Classification","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381412","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"In nature, the morphological changes that occur as cognitive development takes place in human beings and animals have been shown to facilitate learning. Taking inspiration from nature, morphological development aimed at improving learning has been applied in the literature to different robotics configurations and tasks with mixed results. In this paper, we consider a growth-based morphological development approach applied to a quadruped walking task, and, in addition to determining that it improves the learning characteristics, we show that morphological development is something other than just introducing noise as a fitness landscape smoothing technique. Finally, by means of a study of the evolution of the different fitness landscapes, we argue and discuss that learning using growth is equivalent to an incremental learning or fitness landscape shaping approach.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Naya, Martin","Faina, Andres","Duro, Richard"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382672"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Spain"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Universidade da Coruña","University of A Coruna"],"keyword":["Cognitive development","Fitness Landscape","Incremental learning","Morphological Development","Quadruped Robotics"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"growth based morphological development a natural approach to fitness landscape shaping","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/92081791/2022_GECCO_Boston_Letter0.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382672","ids":["itu-5dbe850a-089a-403e-ab41-cef4b0670ec1"],"keyword":["Cognitive Development","Fitness Landscape","Incremental Learning","Morphological Development","Quadruped Robotics"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-5-9D8A4FD35BAB3FD1859C2CD1389F961F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/92081791/2022_GECCO_Boston_Letter0.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"es","key":"university of a coruna","loc":["Spain","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of A Coruna","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"es","key":"universidade da coruña","loc":["Spain","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Universidade da Coruña","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Martin","key":"nayapau","last":"Naya","name":"Naya, Martin","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Richard","key":"duropau","last":"Duro","name":"Duro, Richard","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3520304","eisbn":["9781450392686"],"pages":"140-143","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the Genetic and Evolutionary Computation Conference Companion"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85136332234"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-5dbe850a-089a-403e-ab41-cef4b0670ec1"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Growth-based morphological development: a natural approach to fitness landscape shaping","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382672","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Online teaching, which has been the only way to keep teaching during the pandemic, imposes severe restrictions on hand-on robotic courses. Robot simulators can help to reduce the impact, but most of them use high-level commands to control the robots. In this paper, a new holistic simulator, HoRoSim, is presented. The new simulator uses abstractions of electronic circuits, Arduino code and a robot simulator with a physics engine to simulate microcontroller-based robots. To the best of our knowledge, it is the only tool that simulates Arduino code and multi-body physics. In order to illustrate its possibilities, two use cases are analysed: a line-following robot, which was used by our students to finish their mandatory activities, and a PID controller testbed. Preliminary tests with master's students indicate that HoRoSim can help to increase student engagement during online courses.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Faina, Andres"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382609"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Arduino Simulation","Online teaching","Physics Engine","Robot Simulators","Robotics Education"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"horosim a holistic robot simulator arduino code electronic circuits and physics","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86211605/2021RIE_HoRoSim_camera_ready.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382609","ids":["itu-5a783546-923d-4a8b-ae00-65c1e6ec2ee0"],"keyword":["Arduino Simulation","Online Teaching","Physics Engine","Robot Simulators","Robotics Education"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-F37ABD58410553C9F2A719E778E0A1B3"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86211605/2021RIE_HoRoSim_camera_ready.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-030-82544-7_24","eisbn":["9783030825447"],"isbn":["9783030825430"],"issn":["21945357"],"publisher":"Springer","series":"Advances in Intelligent Systems and Computing","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 12th International Conference on Robotics in Education"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-5a783546-923d-4a8b-ae00-65c1e6ec2ee0"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"HoRoSim, a Holistic Robot Simulator: Arduino Code, Electronic Circuits and Physics","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382609","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Liquid-liquid extraction is a well-known and widely used process in chemistry and laboratory settings. The manual version of this process relies on visual inspection of the mixture, looking for interface formation and stabilization, and for draining the liquids separately. Different automatic devices have been developed for the purpose of separating immiscible liquids, however these solutions are often designed with a specific chemical process in mind. An automatically adjustable LLE device, that can detect and adapt to different substances can shorten adjustment time and enable automatic experimentation in laboratory settings. These kinds of devices are a key part of AI orchestrated, automatic experimentation and screening systems. To enable adaptation in a robust way, an interface detection setup that is able to detect a range of interfaces is presented in this work. The use of a light sensor, that encompasses an array of photo detectors for different light wavelengths, and an arrangement that takes advantage of the optical properties of liquids is investigated for detecting different interfaces with varying optical features. Light sensors have the advantage of not coming in contact with the chemicals involved, as they can be located outside glass vessels. An array of simple sensors reduces the complexity of the signals to be analyzed while at the same time providing enough relevant information to feed more elaborated detection techniques, i.e., machine learning approaches. A dataset is created from sensor measures, characterized, and related to different interface features. Data is automatically generated using a normal glass separatory funnel, connected to a peristaltic pump for controlling the level of liquid inside, but the setup can be adapted for different kinds of vessels and existing automation tools. An analysis of light refraction and reflection inside the funnel is performed. The light source is located on the opposite side to the light sensor and the whole setup is shielded from external light. The dataset obtained will allow the creation of machine learning models (e.g. neural networks) to improve the detection capabilities of the sensor and generalize it to not seen before interfaces. This project has received funding from the European Union’s Horizon 2020 research and innovation programme under grant agreement No 957189","author_count":3,"contributors":["Moreno, Rodrigo","Faina, Andres","Støy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381933"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Lab automation","Liquid Liquid Extraction","Photoelectric sensor"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"photoelectric sensor setup and characterization for general interface detection in laboratory scale liquid liquid extraction processes","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86275868/Poster.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-381933","ids":["itu-2d4aa607-ca39-4b12-aa4d-e07fb243517d"],"keyword":["Lab automation","Liquid-liquid extraction","Photoelectric sensor"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-6043078A5697BD054670E83971A02C57"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86275868/Poster.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rodrigo Moreno","key":"garciapau","last":"Garcia","locid":"5c54c44a-9eaa-4a97-a78a-e853dc73e9d9","name":"Moreno, Rodrigo","name_org":"Garcia, Rodrigo Moreno","orcid":"0000-0003-3738-010X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-2d4aa607-ca39-4b12-aa4d-e07fb243517d"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Photoelectric sensor setup and characterization for general interface detection in laboratory scale liquid-liquid extraction processes","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381933","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"The body of robots and their controllers need to be adapted to the task that they carry out. While it is possible to design and optimize free-form morphologies, its physical implementation consumes too many resources. In contrast, modular robots provide a feasible approach to design robotic morphologies that can be deployed in minutes, making them a suitable tool to implement virtual creatures. In this article, we tackle the main challenges to consider when evolving modular robots and mention some opportunities that these systems can provide.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Faina, Andres"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384420"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Evolutionary Robotics","Modular Robots","Robotic Morphologies","Task specific Adaptation","Virtual creatures"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"evolving modular robots challenges and opportunities","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1162/isal_a_00437","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384420","ids":["itu-d647ce4e-1148-43d8-b878-151dc4c60ede"],"keyword":["Evolutionary Robotics","Modular Robots","Robotic Morphologies","Task-specific Adaptation","Virtual Creatures"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-549EA26FBEE9F6312349AFD5B5BD3B70"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86211567/2021Alife_evolving_modular_robots_keynote_postprint.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1162/isal_a_00437","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1162/isal_a_00437","publisher":"MIT Press","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the ALIFE 2021: The 2021 Conference on Artificial Life"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-d647ce4e-1148-43d8-b878-151dc4c60ede"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Evolving Modular Robots: Challenges and Opportunities","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384420","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Humans and animals undergo morphological development processes from infancy to adulthood that have been shown to facilitate learning. However, most of the work on developmental robotics considers fixed morphologies, addressing only the development of the cognitive system of the robots. This paper aims to provide a survey of the work that is being carried out within the relatively new field of morphological development in robots. In particular, it contemplates morphological development as the changes that occur in the properties of the joints, links and sensors of a robot during its lifetime and focuses on the work carried out by different authors to try to determine their influence on robot learning. To this end, walking, reaching, grasping and vocalization have been identified as the four most representative tasks addressed in the field, clustering the work of the different authors around them. The approach followed is multidisciplinary, discussing the relationships among developmental robotics, embodied artificial intelligence and developmental psychology in humans in general, as well as for each of the tasks, and providing an overview of the many avenues of research that are still open in this field.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Naya, Martin","Faina, Andres","Duro, Richard"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382985"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Spain"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Universidade da Coruña","University of A Coruna"],"keyword":["Cognitive robotics","Morphological Development","Robot learning","Sensory motor coordination"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"morphological development in robotic learning a survey","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86395455/2020_IEEE_Transactions_Naya_et_al_postprint.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382985","identifier":[{"id":"85099731332","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-744cb4ad-57ec-45ec-9c72-28cd0f3ad357"],"keyword":["cognitive robotics","morphological development","robot learning","sensory‐motor coordination"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-9985307024D97205FA0BC5E0F31029BA"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86395455/2020_IEEE_Transactions_Naya_et_al_postprint.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"es","key":"university of a coruna","loc":["Spain","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of A Coruna","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"es","key":"universidade da coruña","loc":["Spain","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Universidade da Coruña","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Martin","key":"nayapau","last":"Naya","name":"Naya, Martin","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Richard","key":"duropau","last":"Duro","name":"Duro, Richard","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/tcds.2021.3052548","issn":["23798920","23798939"],"journal":"IEEE Transactions on Cognitive and Developmental Systems","publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"IEEE Transactions on Cognitive and Developmental Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85099731332"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-744cb4ad-57ec-45ec-9c72-28cd0f3ad357"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Morphological Development in robotic learning: A survey","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382985","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Morphological development has shown its efficiency in improving learning and adaptation to the environment in natural organisms from infancy to adulthood. In the case of robot learning, this is not so clear. The results of a series of experiments that have been carried out in previous work have allowed us to extract, from an analytical perspective, some notions about how and under what conditions morphological development may influence learning. In this paper, we want to adopt an engineering or synthesis perspective and test whether these notions can be used to construct a successful morphological development strategy for a difficult task: learning bipedal locomotion. In particular, we have addressed learning to walk in a 14 degrees of freedom NAO type robot and have designed a morphological development strategy to this end. The results obtained have allowed us to validate the relevance of the assumptions made for the design and implementation of a morphological development strategy.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Naya, Martin","Faina, Andres","Duro, Richard"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384762"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Spain"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Universidade da Coruña","University of A Coruna"],"keyword":["Bipedal locomotion","Learning strategy","Morphological Development","NAO robot","Robot learning"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"learning bipedal walking through morphological development","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86211693/2021_HAIS_Learning_biped_MD_postprint.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384762","identifier":[{"id":"85115826957","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-ed1c415a-2f97-4097-bbc7-6708260392a2"],"keyword":["Bipedal locomotion","Learning strategy","Morphological development","NAO robot","Robot learning"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-6E83E05C12EEC7CB0AD72F66E193FC6A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86211693/2021_HAIS_Learning_biped_MD_postprint.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"es","key":"university of a coruna","loc":["Spain","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of A Coruna","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"es","key":"universidade da coruña","loc":["Spain","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Universidade da Coruña","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Martin","key":"nayapau","last":"Naya","name":"Naya, Martin","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Richard","key":"duropau","last":"Duro","name":"Duro, Richard","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-030-86271-8_16","eisbn":["9783030862718"],"isbn":["9783030862701"],"issn":["03029743"],"publisher":"Springer","series":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 16th International Conference on Hybrid Artificial Intelligence Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85115826957"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-ed1c415a-2f97-4097-bbc7-6708260392a2"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Learning Bipedal Walking Through Morphological Development","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384762","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"This work presents a platform for evolution of morphology in full cycle reconfigurable hardware: The EMERGE (Easy Modular Embodied Robot Generator) modular robot platform. Three parts necessary to implement a full cycle process, i.e., assembling the modules in morphologies, testing the morphologies, disassembling modules and repeating, are described as a previous step to testing a fully autonomous system: the mechanical design of the EMERGE module, extensive tests of the modules by first assembling them manually, and automatic assembly and disassembly tests. EMERGE modules are designed to be easy and fast to build, one module is built in half an hour and is constructed from off-the-shelf and 3D printed parts. Thanks to magnetic connectors, modules are quickly attached and detached to assemble and reconfigure robot morphologies. To test the performance of real EMERGE modules, 30 different morphologies are evolved in simulation, transferred to reality, and tested 10 times. Manual assembly of these morphologies is aided by a visual guiding tool that uses AprilTag markers to check the real modules positions in the morphology against their simulated counterparts and provides a color feedback. Assembly time takes under 5 min for robots with fewer than 10 modules and increases linearly with the number of modules in the morphology. Tests show that real EMERGE morphologies can reproduce the performance of their simulated counterparts, considering the reality gap. Results also show that magnetic connectors allow modules to disconnect in case of being subjected to high external torques that could damage them otherwise. Module tracking combined with their easy assembly and disassembly feature enable EMERGE modules to be also reconfigured using an external robotic manipulator. Experiments demonstrate that it is possible to attach and detach modules from a morphology, as well as release the module from the manipulator using a passive magnetic gripper. This shows that running a completely autonomous, evolution of morphology in full cycle reconfigurable hardware of different topologies for robots is possible and on the verge of being realized. We discuss EMERGE features and the trade-off between reusability and morphological variability among different approaches to physically implement evolved robots.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Moreno, Rodrigo","Faina, Andres"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384959"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Evolutionary Robotics","Morphology evolution","automatic reconfiguration","autonomous hardware evolution","physical robots"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"emerge modular robot a tool for fast deployment of evolved robots","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/frobt.2021.699814","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384959","identifier":[{"id":"85110657301","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-fadaa143-138d-4902-a755-2623c62e8ec8"],"keyword":["automatic reconfiguration","autonomous hardware evolution","evolutionary robotics","morphology evolution","physical robots"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-31DF23358C91ED0B7831BFFB70833974"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86211573/Moreno2021Emerge_Frontiers_published.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/frobt.2021.699814","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rodrigo","key":"morenopau","last":"Moreno","locid":"5c54c44a-9eaa-4a97-a78a-e853dc73e9d9","name":"Moreno, Rodrigo","orcid":"0000-0003-3738-010X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.3389/frobt.2021.699814","issn":["22969144"],"journal":"Frontiers in Robotics and AI","publisher":"Frontiers","status":"p","title":"Frontiers in Robotics and AI","volume":"8"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85110657301"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-fadaa143-138d-4902-a755-2623c62e8ec8"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"EMERGE Modular Robot: A Tool for Fast Deployment of Evolved Robots","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384959","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Natural beings undergo a morphological development process of their bodies while they are learning and adapting to the environments they face from infancy to adulthood. In fact, this is the period where the most important learning processes, those that will support learning as adults, will take place. However, in artificial systems, this interaction between morphological development and learning, and its possible advantages, have seldom been considered. In this line, this paper seeks to provide some insights into how morphological development can be harnessed in order to facilitate learning in embodied systems facing tasks or domains that are hard to learn. In particular, here we will concentrate on whether morphological development can really provide any advantage when learning complex tasks and whether its relevance towards learning increases as tasks become harder. To this end, we present the results of some initial experiments on the application of morphological development to learning to walk in three cases, that of a quadruped, a hexapod and that of an octopod. These results seem to confirm that as task learning difficulty increases the application of morphological development to learning becomes more advantageous.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Naya, Martin","Faina, Andres","Duro, Richard"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384727"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Spain"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Universidade da Coruña","University of A Coruna"],"keyword":["Artificial Systems","Embodied Systems","Learning processes","Morphological Development","Task complexity"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"some experiments on the influence of problem hardness in morphological development based learning of neural controllers","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85513787/2020hais_preprint.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-384727","identifier":[{"id":"85097059677","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-ea878efa-4f8c-48c3-bcfe-9a4e4e72d680"],"keyword":["Artificial Systems","Embodied Systems","Learning Processes","Morphological Development","Task Complexity"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-8F3C866003A3565B137A927F16E7859B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85513787/2020hais_preprint.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"es","key":"university of a coruna","loc":["Spain","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of A Coruna","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"es","key":"universidade da coruña","loc":["Spain","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Universidade da Coruña","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Martin","key":"nayapau","last":"Naya","name":"Naya, Martin","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Richard","key":"duropau","last":"Duro","name":"Duro, Richard","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-030-61705-9_30","eisbn":["9783030617059"],"isbn":["9783030617042"],"issn":["03029743"],"pages":"362-373","publisher":"Springer","series":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","status":"p","subtitle":"HAIS2020","title":"Proceedings of the 15th International Conference on Hybrid Artificial Intelligence Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85097059677"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-ea878efa-4f8c-48c3-bcfe-9a4e4e72d680"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Some Experiments on the influence of Problem Hardness in Morphological Development based Learning of Neural Controllers","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384727","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Commercial liquid handling robots are rarely appropriate when tasks change often, which is the case in the early stages of biochemical research. In order to address it, we have developed EvoBot, a liquid handling robot, which is open-source and employs a modular design. The combination of an open-source and a modular design is particularly powerful because functionality is divided into modules with simple, well-defined interfaces, hence customisation of modules is possible without detailed knowledge of the entire system. Furthermore, the modular design allows end-users to only produce and assemble the modules that are relevant for their specific application. Hence, time and money are not wasted on functionality that is not needed. Finally, modules can easily be reused. In this paper, we describe the EvoBot modular design and through scientific experiments such as basic liquid handling, nurturing of microbial fuel cells, and droplet chemotaxis experiments document how functionality is increased one module at a time with a significant amount of reuse. In addition to providing wet-labs with an extendible, open-source liquid handling robot, we also think that modularity is a key concept that is likely to be useful in other robots developed for scientific purposes.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Faina, Andres","Nejati, Brian","Støy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384598"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["Canada"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Toronto"],"keyword":["Droplets","Evolutionary algorithms","Liquid handling robots","Microbial fuel cells","Modular Robots","Open source robots"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"evobot an open source modular liquid handling robot for scientific experiments","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/app10030814","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384598","identifier":[{"id":"85081599621","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-e1f88b1b-fcd2-4e93-a144-042a7faf0927"],"keyword":["Droplets","Evolutionary Algorithms","Liquid handling robots","Microbial Fuel Cells","Modular Robots","Open-source robots"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-F33652451957EA5363DFA86866F1D041"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/app10030814","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85152786/applsci_10_00814_v3.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"ca","key":"university of toronto","loc":["Canada","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Toronto","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Brian","key":"nejatipau","last":"Nejati","name":"Nejati, Brian","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.3390/app10030814","issn":["20763417"],"issue":"3","journal":"Applied Sciences","paperid":"814","publisher":"Balkan Society of Geometers","status":"p","title":"Applied Sciences","volume":"10"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85081599621"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-e1f88b1b-fcd2-4e93-a144-042a7faf0927"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"EvoBot: An Open-Source, Modular, Liquid Handling Robot for Scientific Experiments","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384598","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"In modular robots, the shape of the building blocks (robotic modules) greatly influences the end result. By changing the physical properties of the module, different robotic structures with better performance for a given task can be found. In this paper, we modify the modules of a modular robot platform, the EMERGE modular robot, in two different ways: changing the length of the module and changing the shape of the starting module (base). We use artificial evolution to optimize robots for a locomotion task using each different module length and base, and also evolve robots with combinations of modules of different length. Results show that, as the length of the module increases, the best robots obtained use fewer modules and fewer connections per module. However, the increase in length results also in a decrease in locomotion performance for large length increases. Interestingly, very few of the best robots found show symmetric structures, which can be attributed to their tendency to roll over as their main means of locomotion. Modular robot designers can use the information about the effectiveness of modules with different lengths, and the use of different starting bases, to reach trade-offs between the desired number of modules in a robot and their effectiveness for a given task.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Moreno, Rodrigo","Faina, Andres"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383907"],"event":{"acronym":"EvoApplications","title":"European Conference on the Applications of Evolutionary Computation"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, South","OECD"],"country":["Colombia"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","National University of Colombia"],"keyword":["Design optimization","Evolutionary algorithms","Modular Robots"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"using evolution to design modular robots an empirical approach to select module designs","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85152903/Paper_Evostar_1_.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383907","identifier":[{"id":"85084740500","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-b524a1de-ae2d-417b-bd3e-2154ae0516c4"],"keyword":["Design optimization","Evolutionary algorithms","Modular robots"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-1E7114A8DD23EABDB2C44B6C364AFFEF"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85152903/Paper_Evostar_1_.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"co","key":"national university of colombia","loc":["Colombia","America, South","OECD"],"name":"National University of Colombia","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rodrigo","key":"morenopau","last":"Moreno","name":"Moreno, Rodrigo","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-030-43722-0_18","eisbn":["9783030437220"],"isbn":["9783030437213"],"issn":["03029743"],"pages":"276-290","publisher":"Springer","series":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","status":"p","title":"Applications of Evolutionary Computation"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85084740500"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-b524a1de-ae2d-417b-bd3e-2154ae0516c4"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Using Evolution to Design Modular Robots: An Empirical Approach to Select Module Designs","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383907","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"The paper introduces a fully automated cultivation and monitoring tool to study biofilm development in replicate experiments operated in parallel. To gain a fundamental understanding of the relation between cultivation conditions and biofilm characteristics (e.g., structural, mechanical) a monitoring setup allowing for the standardization of methods is required. Optical coherence tomography (OCT) is an imaging modality ideal for biofilms since it allows for the monitoring of structure in real time. By integrating an OCT device into the open-source robotic platform EvoBot, a fully automated monitoring platform for investigating biofilm development in several flow cells at once was realized. Different positioning scenarios were tested and revealed that the positioning accuracy is within the optical resolution of the OCT. On that account, a reliable and accurate monitoring of biofilm development by means of OCT has become possible. With this robotic platform, reproducible biofilm experiments including a statistical analysis are achievable with only a small investment of operator time. Furthermore, a number of structural parameters calculated within this study confirmed the necessity to perform replicate biofilm cultivations.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Gierl, Luisa","Støy, Kasper","Faina, Andres","Horn, Harald","Wagner, Michael"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384931"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Karlsruhe Institute of Technology","Karlsruher Institut für Technologie"],"keyword":["Automated cultivation","Biofilm monitoring","Optical coherence tomography","Robotic platforms","Structural biofilm analysis"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"an open source robotic platform that enables automated monitoring of replicate biofilm cultivations using optical coherence tomography","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41522-020-0129-y","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384931","identifier":[{"id":"85082979690","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-f8bd4f54-bc25-45e3-9574-e98971f85ecc"],"keyword":["Automated cultivation","Biofilm monitoring","Optical coherence tomography","Robotic platforms","Structural biofilm analysis"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-6BA96D798E8B2ACDE0B320BE799DF6F7"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85152853/s41522_020_0129_y.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41522-020-0129-y","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"karlsruhe institute of technology","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Karlsruhe Institute of Technology","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, Machine Learning","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"karlsruher institut für technologie","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Karlsruher Institut für Technologie","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Luisa","key":"gierlpau","last":"Gierl","name":"Gierl, Luisa","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Harald","key":"hornpau","last":"Horn","name":"Horn, Harald","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"wagnerpau","last":"Wagner","name":"Wagner, Michael","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41522-020-0129-y","issn":["20555008"],"journal":"npj Biofilms and Microbiomes","paperid":"18","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"npj Biofilms and Microbiomes","volume":"6"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85082979690"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-f8bd4f54-bc25-45e3-9574-e98971f85ecc"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"An open-source robotic platform that enables automated monitoring of replicate biofilm cultivations using optical coherence tomography","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384931","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Few evolved robots have been tested outside simulation due to real world experiments being resource and time expensive. In this paper, we discuss different approaches to physically implement evolved robots and propose a modular robot system with external automatic reconfiguration. While the morphological space is reduced, it provides us with a fast, reusable, fully autonomous system to evolve physical robots in reality.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Moreno, Rodrigo","Faina, Andres"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381277"],"event":{"acronym":"GECCO","title":"Genetic and Evolutionary Computation Conference"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Autonomous System","automatic reconfiguration","evolved robots","modular robot system","physical implementation"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"reusability vs morphological space in physical robot evolution","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3377929.3398135","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381277","identifier":[{"id":"85089732740","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-018e2706-2538-4f93-b976-d849903103aa"],"keyword":["automatic reconfiguration","autonomous system","evolved robots","modular robot system","physical implementation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-7C8AC3E1BF0858B4BF84E96BDE8E3EFA"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85340652/2020_gecco_author_version.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3377929.3398135","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Rodrigo","key":"morenopau","last":"Moreno","name":"Moreno, Rodrigo","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3377929.3398135","isbn":["9781450371278"],"pages":"1389-1391","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 2020 Genetic and Evolutionary Computation Conference Companion"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85089732740"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-018e2706-2538-4f93-b976-d849903103aa"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Reusability vs Morphological Space in Physical Robot Evolution","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381277","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Morphological development is part of the way any human or animal learns. The learning processes starts with the morphology at birth and progresses through changing morphologies until adulthood is reached. Biologically, this seems to facilitate learning and make it more robust. However, when this approach is transferred to robotic systems, the results found in the literature are inconsistent: morphological development does not provide a learning advantage in every case. In fact, it can lead to poorer results than when learning with a fixed morphology. In this paper we analyze some of the issues involved by means of a simple, but very informative experiment in quadruped walking. From the results obtained an initial series of insights on when and under what conditions to apply morphological development for learning are presented.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Naya, Martin","Faina, Andres","Duro, Richard"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381601"],"event":{"acronym":"IJCNN","title":"International Joint Conference on Neural Networks"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Spain"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Universidade da Coruña","University of A Coruna"],"keyword":["Adaptive Morphologies","Biological Learning Processes","Morphological Development","Quadruped Walking Experiment","Robotic Learning"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"an experiment in morphological development for learning ann based controllers","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85513775/2020_experiment_MD_WCCI_preprint_version.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-381601","identifier":[{"id":"85093853504","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-15dd0b11-71b4-45cc-909d-a672ffcaf54f"],"keyword":["Adaptive Morphologies","Biological Learning Processes","Morphological Development","Quadruped Walking Experiment","Robotic Learning"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-AFD4F8ABD82C9F4EDFE03BD59AD15F3C"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85513775/2020_experiment_MD_WCCI_preprint_version.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"es","key":"university of a coruna","loc":["Spain","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of A Coruna","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"es","key":"universidade da coruña","loc":["Spain","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Universidade da Coruña","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Martin","key":"nayapau","last":"Naya","name":"Naya, Martin","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Richard","key":"duropau","last":"Duro","name":"Duro, Richard","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/ijcnn48605.2020.9206749","eisbn":["9781728169262"],"publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of 2020 International Joint Conference on Neural Networks (IJCNN)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85093853504"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-15dd0b11-71b4-45cc-909d-a672ffcaf54f"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"An Experiment in Morphological Development for Learning ANN Based Controllers","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381601","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Microbial Fuel Cells (MFCs) are bio-electrochemical devices that convert organic matter into electricity through bacterial digestion and respiration [1]. MFCs, unlike a battery, can generate electricity continuously as long as fuel is supplied. This has made MFCs an attractive alternative source of electricity for low-power robots. Robots are powered via conventional mains or battery means but this is also a bottleneck for wider deployment; it is therefore envisaged that green energy sources could be one way of powering the next generation of truly autonomous robots [2]. MFCs have previously been reported to power mobile robotic systems such as; Gastrobot, the four families of EcoBot (I, II, III and IV) and Row-bot (a floating robot). However, in order for MFCs to be implemented into the next generation of autonomous agents, their power output capability needs to be improved. This was pursued using a liquid handling robot called EvoBot [3], which interacted with MFCs using a feedback loop mechanism. The real-time electrical output of the MFCs was monitored and fed back to the robot, which was triggering the robotic liquid handling head to supply fuel, in the event of underperformance. This self-managed maintenance mechanism that prompted a feeding “on demand” was able to accelerate the maturing process of the MFCs from 30 down to 6 days. Notably, it also increased the power output by almost 40% compared to the output produced by the manually maintained MFCs on the bench (19.1 mWm-2 to 26.5 mWm-2). This experiment showed the potential of using EvoBot as a maintenance machine for optimising MFCs which can then power autonomous robots.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Theodosiou, Pavlina","Mendis, A.","Faina, Andres","Støy, Kasper","Greenman, John","Melhuish, Chris","Ieropoulos, Ioannis"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382291"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of the West of England"],"keyword":["Autonomous robots","Bio electrochemical devices","Green energy","Liquid handling robot EvoBot","Microbial Fuel Cells MFCs"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"evobot in service interface and interconnection of microbial fuel cells mfcs with a liquid handling robot for optimised performance","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85152955/ISMET_7_Pavlina_Abstract_iai.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382291","ids":["itu-44e74f53-1ed0-403c-88aa-f6acdae2ffff"],"keyword":["Autonomous robots","Bio-electrochemical devices","Green energy","Liquid handling robot EvoBot","Microbial Fuel Cells (MFCs)"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-882D54CD261032D972999DCA136A25E9"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85152955/ISMET_7_Pavlina_Abstract_iai.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"gb","key":"university of the west of england","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of the West of England","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Pavlina","key":"theodosioupau","last":"Theodosiou","name":"Theodosiou, Pavlina","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"A.","key":"mendispau","last":"Mendis","name":"Mendis, A.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"John","key":"greenmanpau","last":"Greenman","name":"Greenman, John","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Chris","key":"melhuishpau","last":"Melhuish","name":"Melhuish, Chris","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ioannis","key":"ieropoulospau","last":"Ieropoulos","name":"Ieropoulos, Ioannis","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-44e74f53-1ed0-403c-88aa-f6acdae2ffff"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"EvoBot in service: interface and interconnection of Microbial Fuel Cells (MFCs) with a liquid handling robot for optimised performance","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382291","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"We have developed a robotic-system capable of performing routine liquid-handling experiments, as well as artificial chemical life experiments. Our platform consists of an actuation-layer on top, an experimental-layer in the middle, and a sensing-layer at the bottom. The actuation-layer comprises the robot-head and modules mounted on it. The modules, e.g. pipet-modules, OCT-scanner, extruder, PH-probe, are designed to perform actions on experiments. The head holds modules and moves in the horizontal plane. The experimental-layer holds the reaction vessels. The sensing-layer consists of a camera below the experimental-layer to monitor the experiment. It collects data from experiment, and provides feedback for robot to interact with experiment.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Nejatimoharrami, Farzad","Faina, Andres","Støy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384016"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Artificial chemical life","Automated experimentation","Multi layer platform","Robotic liquid handling","Sensor feedback systems"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a low cost open source cloud based liquid handling robotic platform for performing remote real time collaborative experiments","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84069536/slas_2018.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384016","ids":["itu-bc363a50-a4b8-41de-af02-cf584ff04c6b"],"keyword":["Artificial chemical life","Automated experimentation","Multi-layer platform","Robotic liquid-handling","Sensor feedback systems"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-6552015A3E0124BC59C993F3DC62C9D1"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://webinars.slas.org/products/a-low-cost-open-source-cloud-based-liquid-handling-robotic-platform-for-performing-remote-real-time-collaborative-experiments#tab-product_tab_overview","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84069536/slas_2018.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Farzad","key":"nejatimoharramipau","last":"Nejatimoharrami","name":"Nejatimoharrami, Farzad","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-bc363a50-a4b8-41de-af02-cf584ff04c6b"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"A Low-Cost Open-Source Cloud-based Liquid Handling Robotic Platform for Performing Remote Real-Time Collaborative Experiments","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384016","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Self-reconfigurability in modular robots is a challenging task that usually requires complex module designs and algorithms. To make the reconfigurability easier, unpowered permanent magnet-based modules can be reconfigured by an external robotic manipulator. These modules are, thereby, not dependent on each separate module’s inclusion of batteries and control circuitry since they can be reconfigured in their unpowered state, and the reconfiguration movements are not restricted to the kinematic chain of the modular robot. In this paper, we discuss how we can utilize an active or passive gripper to assemble and disassemble modular robots. We furthermore demonstrate that the use of fiducial markers allows the robot arm to accurately pick and place the modules to reconfigure the morphologies. The utilization of robotic arms and a visual feedback system allows us to quickly create robot morphologies from modules, which can be evaluated in the real world and then reshaped using the same components. This technique is especially valuable to enable the rapid generation and evaluation of robot morphologies in the real world that would traditionally need to be reconstructed or reassembled with complex modules or complex attachment mechanisms.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Moreno, Rodrigo","Veenstra, Frank","Silvera, David","Franco, Julian","Gracia, Oscar","Cordoba, Ernesto","Gomez, Jonatan","Faina, Andres"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382497"],"event":{"acronym":"IEEE SSCI","title":"IEEE Symposium Series on Computational Intelligence"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, South","OECD"],"country":["Colombia"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","National University of Colombia"],"keyword":["Fiducial markers","Modular robotics","Permanent magnets","Robotic manipulation","Self reconfigurable robots"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"automated reconfiguration of modular robots using robot manipulators","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83599695/automated_assembly_modular_final.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382497","identifier":[{"id":"85062769795","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-523a6687-b39a-4189-985b-e207e1bda62b"],"keyword":["Fiducial markers","Modular robotics","Permanent magnets","Robotic manipulation","Self-reconfigurable robots"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-C7C2D8500A2A09B1B2E7E87864A183FE"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83599695/automated_assembly_modular_final.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"co","key":"national university of colombia","loc":["Colombia","America, South","OECD"],"name":"National University of Colombia","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Rodrigo","key":"morenopau","last":"Moreno","name":"Moreno, Rodrigo","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Frank","key":"veenstrapau","last":"Veenstra","name":"Veenstra, Frank","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"David","key":"silverapau","last":"Silvera","name":"Silvera, David","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Julian","key":"francopau","last":"Franco","name":"Franco, Julian","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Oscar","key":"graciapau","last":"Gracia","name":"Gracia, Oscar","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Ernesto","key":"cordobapau","last":"Cordoba","name":"Cordoba, Ernesto","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jonatan","key":"gomezpau","last":"Gomez","name":"Gomez, Jonatan","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/ssci.2018.8628628","eisbn":["9781538692769"],"isbn":["9781538692752"],"pages":"884-891","place":"Bangalore, India","publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 2018 IEEE Symposium Series on Computational Intelligence (SSCI 2018)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85062769795"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-523a6687-b39a-4189-985b-e207e1bda62b"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Automated Reconfiguration of Modular Robots Using Robot Manipulators","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382497","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"A modular robot can be reconfigured and reorganized to perform different tasks. Due to the large number of configurations that this type of robot can have, several types of techniques have been developed to generate locomotion tasks in an adaptive manner. One of these techniques transfers sets of parameters to the robot controller from a simulation. However, in most cases the simulated approach is not appropriate, since it does not take into account all physical interactions between the robot and the environment. This paper shows the design of a flexible controller that adapts to the different configurations of a modular chain-type robot, which coordinates the movements of the robot using a Central Pattern Generator (CPG). The CPG is integrated with an optimization algorithm to estimate sets of movements, which allow the robot to navigate in its environment autonomously from the information of sensors and in real time.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Hernández, Henry","Moreno, Rodrigo","Faina, Andres","Gomez, Jonatan"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382039"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, South","OECD"],"country":["Colombia"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","National University of Colombia"],"keyword":["Autonomous operation","Genetic algorithm","Modular robot"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"design of a bio inspired controller to operate a modular robot autonomously","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83599717/design_bio_inspired.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382039","identifier":[{"id":"85057083749","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-34ab3a9c-9d61-4b3b-8ac5-0ee7b6e88cd6"],"keyword":["Autonomous operation","Genetic algorithm","Modular robot"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-D8B01673E09B93CFCF1B869789975211"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83599717/design_bio_inspired.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"co","key":"national university of colombia","loc":["Colombia","America, South","OECD"],"name":"National University of Colombia","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Henry","key":"hernándezpau","last":"Hernández","name":"Hernández, Henry","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rodrigo","key":"morenopau","last":"Moreno","name":"Moreno, Rodrigo","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jonatan","key":"gomezpau","last":"Gomez","name":"Gomez, Jonatan","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-030-03928-8_26","eisbn":["9783030039288"],"isbn":["9783030039271"],"issn":["03029743"],"pages":"314-325","publisher":"Springer","series":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","status":"p","subtitle":"IBERAMIA 2018","title":"Ibero-American Conference on Artificial Intelligence"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85057083749"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-34ab3a9c-9d61-4b3b-8ac5-0ee7b6e88cd6"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Design of a Bio-Inspired Controller to Operate a Modular Robot Autonomously","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382039","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"author_count":5,"contributors":["Moreno Garcia, Rodrigo","Liu, Ceyue","Hernandez, Henry","Gomez, Jonatan","Faina, Andres"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381338"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, South","Asia, East","OECD"],"country":["China","Colombia"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","China University of Mining and Technology","National University of Colombia"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"quick testing of evolved robot morphologies using emerge modules","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82063782/quick_testing_emerge.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381338","ids":["itu-05457a1a-9df4-406a-92fd-7da8f84088de"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-7F1BC0265EA8E56757CC1D3FC9BE309D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82063782/quick_testing_emerge.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"co","key":"national university of colombia","loc":["Colombia","America, South","OECD"],"name":"National University of Colombia","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"cn","key":"china university of mining and technology","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"China University of Mining and Technology","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rodrigo","key":"morenogarciapau","last":"Moreno Garcia","name":"Moreno Garcia, Rodrigo","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Ceyue","key":"liupau","last":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ceyue","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Henry","key":"hernandezpau","last":"Hernandez","name":"Hernandez, Henry","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jonatan","key":"gomezpau","last":"Gomez","name":"Gomez, Jonatan","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-05457a1a-9df4-406a-92fd-7da8f84088de"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Quick testing of evolved robot morphologies using EMERGE modules","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381338","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"The Robobo Project is a STEM-based project that aims to bring educational robotics, in primary and high school, closer to real-world applications. It is based on the use of a smartphone-based robotic platform called Robobo, a very flexible programming environment, and a set of lessons to integrate them. The smartphone provides high-level hardware capabilities in terms of sensors, communications and processing capabilities that allow to create more practical and realistic lessons that exploit human-robot interaction, with a small investment. In this paper, we present the main elements of The Robobo Project in terms of hardware and software, and two illustrative educational projects that can be developed within it.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Bellas, Francisco","Naya, Martin","Varela, Gervasio","Llamas, Lius","Prieto, Abraham","Becerra, Juan Carlos","Bautista, Moises","Faina, Andres","Duro, Richard"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382116"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Spain"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of A Coruna"],"keyword":["Computer vision","Interactive Education","Real World Robotics","Smartphones","Speech recognition"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the robobo project bringing educational robotics closer to real world applications","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82085470/2017_Rie_postprint.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382116","identifier":[{"id":"85029461421","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-39a6eae2-0f40-46c2-ac4b-52cbe0d8f2fc"],"keyword":["Computer Vision","Interactive Education","Real-World Robotics","Smartphones","Speech Recognition"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-D212D9C43B12891B24C2BADD6227A1CD"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82085470/2017_Rie_postprint.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"es","key":"university of a coruna","loc":["Spain","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of A Coruna","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Francisco","key":"bellaspau","last":"Bellas","name":"Bellas, Francisco","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Martin","key":"nayapau","last":"Naya","name":"Naya, Martin","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Gervasio","key":"varelapau","last":"Varela","name":"Varela, Gervasio","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lius","key":"llamaspau","last":"Llamas","name":"Llamas, Lius","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Abraham","key":"prietopau","last":"Prieto","name":"Prieto, Abraham","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Juan Carlos","key":"becerrapau","last":"Becerra","name":"Becerra, Juan Carlos","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Moises","key":"bautistapau","last":"Bautista","name":"Bautista, Moises","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Richard","key":"duropau","last":"Duro","name":"Duro, Richard","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-319-62875-2_20","isbn":["9783319628752"],"pages":"226-237","publisher":"Springer","series":"Advances in Intelligent Systems and Computing Series","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of Robotics In Education"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85029461421"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-39a6eae2-0f40-46c2-ac4b-52cbe0d8f2fc"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Robobo Project: Bringing Educational Robotics Closer to Real-World Applications","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382116","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"Many different types of modular robots have been designed in the last two decades. However, limited research has been done on analyzing which module morphology is able to create better robots for a given task. To address this issue, this paper investigates how the number and position of available connection faces in a module influence the evolvability of the modular robot. In contrast to previous research on modular robots, an analysis of the morphology of the module is done in order to improve and simplify its mechanical design. To this end, we designed a homogeneous module called EMeRGE, and defined the number of connection faces and their relative positions as morphological parameters. Afterwards, we evolved the morphology and control of robots composed of EMeRGE modules in a robotic simulation platform. Simulation results indicate that robots containing modules with only two available connection faces were able to acquire better performance than robots that contained modules using more connection faces for a locomotion task. Finally, the simulated robots were transferred to the real world in the actual modular robot to verify the simulation results.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Liu, Ceyue","Liu, Jiangong","Moreno Garcia, Rodrigo","Veenstra, Frank","Faina, Andres"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381940"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, South","Asia, East","OECD"],"country":["China","Colombia"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","China University of Mining and Technology","National University of Colombia"],"keyword":["Connection Faces","Evolvability","Modular robotics","Module Morphology","Simulation to Real World Transfer"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the impact of module morphologies on modular robots","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82144275/PID4847001_The_impact_of_module_morphologies_on_modular_robots_.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381940","identifier":[{"id":"85031686486","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-2d8b1670-2d04-4b4c-b632-585d2295b1a2"],"keyword":["Connection Faces","Evolvability","Modular Robotics","Module Morphology","Simulation to Real-World Transfer"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-2E624A1B3B801069C5842A8583A78E00"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82144275/PID4847001_The_impact_of_module_morphologies_on_modular_robots_.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"cn","key":"china university of mining and technology","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"China University of Mining and Technology","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"co","key":"national university of colombia","loc":["Colombia","America, South","OECD"],"name":"National University of Colombia","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ceyue","key":"liuceyuepau","last":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ceyue","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jiangong","key":"liujiangongpau","last":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiangong","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Rodrigo","key":"morenogarciarodrigopau","last":"Moreno Garcia","name":"Moreno Garcia, Rodrigo","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Frank","key":"veenstrafrankpau","last":"Veenstra","name":"Veenstra, Frank","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainaandrespau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/icar.2017.8023524","eisbn":["9781538631577"],"isbn":["9781538631560","9781538631584"],"publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of 18th International Conference on Advanced Robotics (ICAR)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85031686486"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-2d8b1670-2d04-4b4c-b632-585d2295b1a2"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The impact of module morphologies on modular robots","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381940","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"In this paper we present EvoBot, a RepRap open-source 3D-printer modified to operate like a robot for culturing and maintaining Microbial Fuel Cells (MFCs). EvoBot is a modular liquid handling robot that has been adapted to host MFCs in its experimental layer, gather data from the MFCs and react on the set thresholds based on a feedback loop. This type of robot-MFC interaction, based on the feedback loop mechanism, will enable us to study further the adaptability and stability of these systems. To date, EvoBot has automated the nurturing process of MFCs with the aim of controlling liquid delivery, which is akin to a chemostat. The chemostat is a well-known microbiology method for culturing bacterial cells under controlled conditions with continuous nutrient supply. EvoBot is perhaps the first pioneering attempt at functionalizing the 3D printing technology by combining it with the chemostat methods. In this paper, we will explore the experiments that EvoBot has carried out so far and how the platform has been optimised over the past two years.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Theodosiou, Pavlina","Faina, Andres","Nejatimoharrami, Farzad","Støy, Kasper","Greenman, John","Melhuish, Chris","Ieropoulos, Ioannis"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383221"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of the West of England"],"keyword":["3D printer","Cathode","EvoBot","Microbial fuel cells","Rep Rap"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"evobot towards a robot chemostat for culturing and maintaining microbial fuel cells mfcs","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82085446/Evobot_Theodosiou_camera_ready.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383221","identifier":[{"id":"85145778573","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-850b850c-378c-4992-ae13-22ca9c749ba1"],"keyword":["3D-printer","Cathode","EvoBot","Microbial Fuel Cells","Rep-Rap"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-9DBB518EB46B3C9E7A68CC605BDBFB22"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82085446/Evobot_Theodosiou_camera_ready.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2017","yearSub":"2017"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"gb","key":"university of the west of england","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of the West of England","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Pavlina","key":"theodosioupau","last":"Theodosiou","name":"Theodosiou, Pavlina","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Farzad","key":"nejatimoharramipau","last":"Nejatimoharrami","name":"Nejatimoharrami, Farzad","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"John","key":"greenmanpau","last":"Greenman","name":"Greenman, John","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Chris","key":"melhuishpau","last":"Melhuish","name":"Melhuish, Chris","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ioannis","key":"ieropoulospau","last":"Ieropoulos","name":"Ieropoulos, Ioannis","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-319-63537-8_38","eisbn":["9783319635378"],"isbn":["9783319635361"],"issn":["03029743"],"pages":"453-464","publisher":"Springer","series":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","status":"p","subtitle":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","title":"Proceedings of the 6th International Conference Living Machines 2017","volume":"10384"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85145778573"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-850b850c-378c-4992-ae13-22ca9c749ba1"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"EvoBot: Towards a Robot-Chemostat for Culturing and Maintaining Microbial Fuel Cells (MFCs)","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383221","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"Evolutionary robotics is challenged with some key problems that must be solved, or at least mitigated extensively, before it can fulfill some of its promises to deliver highly autonomous and adaptive robots. The reality gap and the ability to transfer phenotypes from simulation to reality constitute one such problem. Another lies in the embodiment of the evolutionary processes, which links to the first, but focuses on how evolution can act on real agents and occur independently from simulation, that is, going from being, as Eiben et al. put it, “the evolution of things, rather than just the evolution of digital objects.…” The work presented here investigates how fully autonomous evolution of robot controllers can be realized in hardware, using an industrial robot and a marker-based computer vision system. In particular, this article presents an approach to automate the reconfiguration of the test environment and shows that it is possible, for the first time, to incrementally evolve a neural robot controller for different obstacle avoidance tasks with no human intervention. Importantly, the system offers a high level of robustness and precision that could potentially open up the range of problems amenable to embodied evolution.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Faina, Andres","Jacobsen, Lars Toft","Risi, Sebastian"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381543"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Evolution in hardware","Evolution of physical systems EPS","Evolutionary Robotics"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"automating the incremental evolution of controllers for physical robots","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82003890/preprint_automating_incremental_evolution.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381543","identifier":[{"id":"85020057944","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-12778aa0-01a5-46ea-b984-17eecfa4fcb4"],"keyword":["Evolution of physical systems (EPS)","evolution in hardware","evolutionary robotics"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-75559F1D68EE5051087507DFD993A61B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82003890/preprint_automating_incremental_evolution.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2017","yearSub":"2017"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Lars Toft","key":"jacobsenpau","last":"Jacobsen","name":"Jacobsen, Lars Toft","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sebastian","key":"risipau","last":"Risi","locid":"a420ffb3-2ca6-4925-a25f-baaa5228fc15","name":"Risi, Sebastian","orcid":"0000-0003-3607-8400","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1162/artl_a_00226","issn":["10645462","15309185"],"issn_org":["10645462"],"issue":"2","journal":"Artificial Life","pages":"142-168","publisher":"MIT Press","status":"p","title":"Artificial Life","volume":"23"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85020057944"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-12778aa0-01a5-46ea-b984-17eecfa4fcb4"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Automating the Incremental Evolution of Controllers for Physical Robots","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381543","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"This work presents the hardware design and implementation of the EMeRGE open modular robot platform. EMeRGE (Easy Modular Embodied Robot Generation) modules are designed to be cheap and easy to build and their hardware is open for anyone to use and modify. Four magnetic connectors enable the quick assembly of different complex robot morphologies like the ones generated by evolutionary robotics experiments. Non-human agents, like robotic manipulators, can also take advantage of the magnetic connectors to assemble and disassemble morphologies.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Moreno Garcia, Rodrigo","Liu, Ceyue","Faina, Andres","Hernandez, Henry","Gomez, Jonatan"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383990"],"event":{"acronym":"GECCO","title":"Genetic and Evolutionary Computation Conference"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, South","Asia, East","OECD"],"country":["China","Colombia"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","China University of Mining and Technology","National University of Colombia"],"keyword":["Magnetic connectors","Modular Robots","Morphology","Open hardware","Quick assembly","Transferability"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the emerge modular robot an open platform for quick testing of evolved robot morphologies","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3067695.3075616","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383990","identifier":[{"id":"85032152081","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-ba32949d-33d8-4d5b-9c8a-82a1c3b08824"],"keyword":["Magnetic connectors","Modular Robots","Morphology","Open hardware","Quick assembly","Transferability"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-6B2C2F4F94814A3452537EB4DFD4BAB6"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3067695.3075616","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82085464/emerge_modular_robot_postprint.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"co","key":"national university of colombia","loc":["Colombia","America, South","OECD"],"name":"National University of Colombia","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"cn","key":"china university of mining and technology","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"China University of Mining and Technology","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rodrigo","key":"morenogarciapau","last":"Moreno Garcia","name":"Moreno Garcia, Rodrigo","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Ceyue","key":"liupau","last":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ceyue","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Henry","key":"hernandezpau","last":"Hernandez","name":"Hernandez, Henry","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jonatan","key":"gomezpau","last":"Gomez","name":"Gomez, Jonatan","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3067695.3075616","eisbn":["9781450349390"],"pages":"71-72","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"GECCO '17 Proceedings of the Genetic and Evolutionary Computation Conference Companion"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-ba32949d-33d8-4d5b-9c8a-82a1c3b08824"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The EMeRGE modular robot, an open platform for quick testing of evolved robot morphologies","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383990","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"We introduce a robot developed to perform feedback-based experiments, such as droplet experiments, a common type of experiments in artificial chemical life research. These experiments are particularly well suited for automation because they often stretch over long periods of time, possibly hours, and often require that the human takes action in response to observed events such as changes in droplet size, count, shape, or clustering or declustering of multiple droplets. Our robot is designed to monitor long-term experiments and, based on the feedback from the experiment, interact with it. The combination of precise automation, accurately collected experiment data, and integrated analysis and modeling software makes real-time interaction with the experiment feasible, as opposed to traditional offline processing of experiments. Last but not least, we believe the low cost of our platform can promote artificial life research. Furthermore, prevalently, findings from an experiment will inspire redesign for novel experiments. In addition, the robot’s open-source software enables easy modification of experiments. We will cover two case studies for application of our robot in feedback-based experiments and demonstrate how our robot can not only automate these experiments, collect data, and interact with the experiments intelligently but also enable chemists to perform formerly infeasible experiments.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Nejatimoharrami, Farzad","Faina, Andres","Støy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384372"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Programming","Robotics and instrumentation","Systems engineering","automated biology","informatics and software"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"new capabilities of evobot a modular open source liquid handling robot","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82993190/8_Nejatimoharrami_2017_New_Capabilities_of_EvoBot.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384372","identifier":[{"id":"85029919460","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-d35ec374-f477-4f94-a6be-11fa893a86d3"],"keyword":["Robotics and instrumentation","automated biology","informatics and software","programming","systems engineering"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-7C5FCFD52732800048827129168FE3D0"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82993190/8_Nejatimoharrami_2017_New_Capabilities_of_EvoBot.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Farzad","key":"nejatimoharramipau","last":"Nejatimoharrami","name":"Nejatimoharrami, Farzad","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/2472630316689285","issn":["24726311"],"journal":"SLAS TECHNOLOGY","pages":"1-7","publisher":"Sage Publishing","status":"p","title":"SLAS TECHNOLOGY"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85029919460"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-d35ec374-f477-4f94-a6be-11fa893a86d3"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"New Capabilities of EvoBot, A Modular, Open-Source Liquid-Handling Robot","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384372","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"author_count":4,"contributors":["Veenstra, Frank","Faina, Andres","Støy, Kasper","Risi, Sebastian"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384612"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"evolving simulated modular robots","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82063888/evolving_simulated_modular_2_.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384612","ids":["itu-e2ffe95a-13e7-4f08-953f-1ab046df2343"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-208C249102894E0C102E06D0723BE104"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/82063888/evolving_simulated_modular_2_.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Frank","key":"veenstrapau","last":"Veenstra","name":"Veenstra, Frank","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sebastian","key":"risipau","last":"Risi","locid":"a420ffb3-2ca6-4925-a25f-baaa5228fc15","name":"Risi, Sebastian","orcid":"0000-0003-3607-8400","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-e2ffe95a-13e7-4f08-953f-1ab046df2343"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Evolving Simulated Modular Robots","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384612","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"Modular robots oer an important benet in evolutionary robotics, which is to quickly evaluate evolved morphologies and control systems in reality. However, articial evolution of simulated modular robotics is a dicult and time consuming task requiring signicant computational power. While articial evolution in virtual creatures has made use of powerful generative encodings, here we investigate how a generative encoding and direct encoding compare for the evolution of locomotion in modular robots when the number of robotic modules changes. Simulating less modules would decrease the size of the genome of a direct encoding while the size of the genome of the implemented generative encoding stays the same. We found that the generative encoding is signi cantly more ecient in creating robot phenotypes in the initial stages of evolution when simulating a maximum of 5, 10, and 20 modules. This not only conrms that generative encodings lead to decent performance more quickly, but also that when simulating just a few modules a generative encoding is more powerful than a direct encoding for creating robotic structures. Over longer evolutionary time, the dierence between the encodings no longer becomes statistically signicant. This leads us to speculate that a combined approach { starting with a generative encoding and later implementing a direct encoding { can lead to more ecient evolved designs.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Veenstra, Frank","Faina, Andres","Risi, Sebastian","Støy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382184"],"event":{"acronym":"EvoApplications","end":"2017-04-21","place":"Amsterdam, nl","start":"2017-04-19","title":"Evostar 2017","year":"2017"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Direct Generative Encodings","Evolutionary algorithms","Modular Robots"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"evolution and morphogenesis of simulated modular robots a comparison between a direct and generative encoding","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/81921390/Veenstra2017_EvolutionAndMorphogenesisofSimulatedModularRobots.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382184","identifier":[{"id":"85017551035","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-3e05d80f-13ca-438f-8722-697a1beda810"],"keyword":["Direct & Generative Encodings","Evolutionary Algorithms","Modular Robots"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-F7119E100722104C4A9A7E083622E1A9"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/81921390/Veenstra2017_EvolutionAndMorphogenesisofSimulatedModularRobots.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2017","yearSub":"2017"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Frank","key":"veenstrapau","last":"Veenstra","name":"Veenstra, Frank","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sebastian","key":"risipau","last":"Risi","locid":"a420ffb3-2ca6-4925-a25f-baaa5228fc15","name":"Risi, Sebastian","orcid":"0000-0003-3607-8400","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-319-55849-3_56","eisbn":["9783319558493"],"isbn":["9783319558486"],"issn":["03029743"],"publisher":"Springer","series":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","status":"p","subtitle":"20th European Conference, EvoApplications 2017, Amsterdam, The Netherlands, April 19-21, 2017, Proceedings, Part I","title":"Applications of Evolutionary Computation"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85017551035"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-3e05d80f-13ca-438f-8722-697a1beda810"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Evolution and Morphogenesis of Simulated Modular Robots: A Comparison Between a Direct and Generative Encoding","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382184","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"We have developed a low-cost, open source robot to automate reactive liquid handling tasks. Specifically, the robot, called EvoBot, targets reactive manipulation of motile droplets, a task prevalent in artificial chemical life research. We address hardware design and software implementation of our robot and demonstrate EvoBot's reactive capability, extensibility, and ability to perform experiments in interaction with the user. EvoBot has a three layer design, 1) a moving head with plug'n'play modules such as syringes on top, 2) a transparent experimental layer, accommodating different chemical vessels in the middle, and 3) a camera as the sensing system at the bottom, providing a view of the experiment. From the raw camera image experiment specific data such as droplet size, position, speed, number, color, and shape are calculated. The computer vision system has an accuracy of 4% for droplet area calculation, and EvoBot an accuracy of 0.1 millimeters in positioning droplets on the experimental layer. EvoBot is able to detect a relevant change of behavior, and accordingly interact with the experiment, e.g. remove a specific droplet by absorbing it. Therefore EvoBot is capable of fully automating experiments, e.g. tracking a moving droplet and injecting a chemical at droplet center when the speed goes below a threshold. Eliminating the need for the presence of a human to perform the interaction is crucial as artificial chemical life experiments are commonly long lasting. EvoBot's application domain is extendable owing to a modular design of hardware, and open source software. Evobot's modular design enables support for different modules, e.g. syringe modules for liquid handling, grippers to reposition reaction vessels or dispose of them, sensor modules including temperature, pH, etc, as well as other potential modules based on experiment needs. EvoBot's application programming interface (API), a programming interface allowing researchers to build on top of the functionality of the robot, along with the computer vision API allow modification of experiments with a limited programming effort. This is significant as in the case of artificial chemical life experiments often findings from experiments inspire experimenters to modify the design of forthcoming experiments. In addition to fully automated experiments, there are experiments that require human robot cooperation. EvoBot supports an interactive mode, where the creativity of a human for novel experiments is coupled with the power of real time accurate computer processed data and precise liquid handling. For instance, EvoBot displays the distance from a clicked point to the center of a droplet on the experiment video, and can inject precisely at the desired point. In summary, due to its precision, EvoBot improves the quality of experimental laboratory data, and empowers chemists to push boundaries of artificial life research by performing formerly infeasible experiments because of their interactive and long lasting nature. Finally, as EvoBot is based on open-source hardware, the cheap price of the robot makes it affordable even for personal labs.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Nejatimoharrami, Farzad","Faina, Andres","Støy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382920"],"event":{"end":"2016-01-27","place":"San Diego, us","start":"2016-01-23","title":"2016 Society for Lab Automation and Screening International Conference &amp; Exhibition","year":"2016"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Artificial chemical life","Computer vision in experiments","Liquid handling automation","Open source hardware","Robotics"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"an open source low cost robot for performing reactive liquid handling experiments","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-382920","ids":["itu-6ee74762-9c83-4c66-8df1-15f1756498d3"],"keyword":["Artificial chemical life","Computer vision in experiments","Liquid handling automation","Open source hardware","Robotics"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-A3ABE0410F8808FECE55F27C98F1A89B"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab","The Department, Software & Systems"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Farzad","key":"nejatimoharramipau","last":"Nejatimoharrami","name":"Nejatimoharrami, Farzad","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-6ee74762-9c83-4c66-8df1-15f1756498d3"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"An Open-Source, Low-Cost Robot for Performing Reactive Liquid Handling Experiments","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382920","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"author_count":3,"contributors":["Nejatimoharrami, Farzad","Faina, Andres","Støy, Kasper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384324"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"robotic automation to augment quality of artificial chemical life experiments","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://mitpress.mit.edu/sites/default/files/titles/free_download/9780262339360_ALIFE_2016.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384324","identifier":[{"id":"85087106333","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-d05519fb-36c3-4664-86ac-dd6a107433d6"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-38EA4606F8BC7B5D557646FCB1CFAFFF"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://mitpress.mit.edu/sites/default/files/titles/free_download/9780262339360_ALIFE_2016.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Farzad","key":"nejatimoharramipau","last":"Nejatimoharrami","name":"Nejatimoharrami, Farzad","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9780262339360"],"pages":"634-635","publisher":"MIT Press","series":"Complex Adaptive Systems","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the Artificial Life Conference 2016"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85087106333"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-d05519fb-36c3-4664-86ac-dd6a107433d6"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Robotic Automation to Augment Quality of Artificial Chemical Life Experiments","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384324","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"Liquid handling robots are rarely used in the domain of artificial life. In this field, transitory behaviours of non-equilibrium man-made systems are studied and need an automatic monitoring and logging of results. In addition, artificial life experiments are dynamic with frequent changes, which makes it difficult to apply conventional liquid handling robots as they are designed to automate a predefined task. In order to address these issues, we have developed an open source liquid handling robot, EvoBot. It uses a modular approach, which gives us the possibility to reconfigure the robot for different experiments and make it possible for users to add functionality by just developing a function specific module. In addition, it provides sensors and extra functionality for monitoring an experiment, which allows researchers to perform interactive experiments with the aim of prolonging non-equilibrium conditions. In this paper, we describe the modular design of EvoBot, document its performance, and provide a novel example of an interactive experiment in artificial life, where the robot nurtures a microbial fuel cell based on its voltage output.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Faina, Andres","Nejatimoharrami, Farzad","Støy, Kasper","Theodosiou, Pavlina","Taylor, Benjamin","Ieropoulos, Ioannis"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381508"],"event":{"end":"2016-07-08","place":"Cancun, mx","start":"2016-07-04","title":"Artificial Life","year":"2016"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of the West of England"],"keyword":["Artificial Life","Liquid handling robots","Microbial fuel cell experimentation","Modular robotics","Non equilibrium systems"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"evobot an open source modular liquid handling robot for nurturing microbial fuel cells","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/81147673/faina2016alife_final.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381508","identifier":[{"id":"85087104378","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-10351349-c4af-4865-8965-d317fddbf1f6"],"keyword":["Artificial life","Liquid handling robots","Microbial fuel cell experimentation","Modular robotics","Non-equilibrium systems"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-9C457B591F8F520797824381A6C7DDE0"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/81147673/faina2016alife_final.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab","The Department, Software & Systems"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"university of the west of england","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of the West of England","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Farzad","key":"nejatimoharramipau","last":"Nejatimoharrami","name":"Nejatimoharrami, Farzad","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Pavlina","key":"theodosioupau","last":"Theodosiou","name":"Theodosiou, Pavlina","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Benjamin","key":"taylorpau","last":"Taylor","name":"Taylor, Benjamin","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Ioannis","key":"ieropoulospau","last":"Ieropoulos","name":"Ieropoulos, Ioannis","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9780262339360"],"pages":"626-633","publisher":"MIT Press","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the Artificial Life Conference 2016"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85087104378"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-10351349-c4af-4865-8965-d317fddbf1f6"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"EvoBot: An Open-Source, Modular Liquid Handling Robot for Nurturing Microbial Fuel Cells","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381508","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"author_count":6,"contributors":["Janská, Petra","Zadražil, Aleš","Nejatimoharrami, Farzad","Faina, Andres","Støy, Kasper","Čejková, Jitka"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383097"],"event":{"end":"2016-05-27","place":"Tatranské Matliare, sk","start":"2016-05-23","title":"43rd Conference of Slovak Society of Chemical Engineering","year":"2016"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Czechia"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Chemistry and Technology Prague"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"collective behaviour in droplet systems","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-383097","ids":["itu-7cbfc7ef-81e0-4fee-94c8-3f67d37549b0"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-BED6284AE818CBDDDA1711EB61456045"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"cz","key":"university of chemistry and technology prague","loc":["Czechia","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Chemistry and Technology Prague","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab","The Department, Software & Systems"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Petra","key":"janskápau","last":"Janská","name":"Janská, Petra","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Aleš","key":"zadražilpau","last":"Zadražil","name":"Zadražil, Aleš","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Farzad","key":"nejatimoharramipau","last":"Nejatimoharrami","name":"Nejatimoharrami, Farzad","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jitka","key":"čejkovápau","last":"Čejková","name":"Čejková, Jitka","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-7cbfc7ef-81e0-4fee-94c8-3f67d37549b0"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Collective behaviour in droplet systems","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383097","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"Due to the replacement of natural flora and fauna with ur- ban environments, a significant part of the earth’s organisms that function as primary consumers have been dispelled. To compensate for the reduction in the amount of primary con- sumers, robotic systems that mimic plant-like organisms are interesting to mimic for their potential functional and aes- thetic value in urban environments. To investigate how to utilize plant developmental strategies in order to engender ur- ban artificial plants, we built a simple evolutionary model that applies an L-System based grammar as an abstraction of plant development. In the presented experiments, phytomorpholo- gies (plant morphologies) are iteratively constructed using a context sensitive L-System. The genomic representation of the L-System is subject to mutation by an evolutionary al- gorithm. These mutations thus alter the developmental rules of these phytomorphologies. We compare the differences be- tween the light absorption of evolving virtual plants that re- main static during their life and virtual plants that possess the possibility to move joints that link the separate parts of the virtual plants. Our results show that our evolutionary al- gorithm did not exploit potential beneficial joint actuation, instead, mostly static structures evolved. The results of our evolving L-System show that it is able to create various phy- tomorphologies, albeit that the results are preliminary and will be more thoroughly investigated in the future","author_count":4,"contributors":["Veenstra, Frank","Faina, Andres","Støy, Kasper","Risi, Sebastian"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382007"],"event":{"end":"2016-07-08","place":"Cancun, mx","start":"2016-07-04","title":"Artificial Life","year":"2016"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Evolutionary Robotics","L Systems","Plant Inspired Robotics","Simulated Evolution"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"generating artificial plant morphologies for function and aesthetics through evolving l systems","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/81213335/Eventual_article_Plant.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382007","identifier":[{"id":"85087106895","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-32dfa560-6838-4b38-8c1f-ccd0a0fcb432"],"keyword":["Evolutionary Robotics","L-Systems","Plant-Inspired Robotics","Simulated Evolution"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-496139302AC1F596981041A15F298CCB"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/81213335/Eventual_article_Plant.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab","The Department, Software & Systems"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Frank","key":"veenstrapau","last":"Veenstra","name":"Veenstra, Frank","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andres","key":"fainapau","last":"Faina","locid":"7dd48cfe-3069-4563-bef4-ef661c18387a","name":"Faina, Andres","orcid":"0000-0001-7288-6988","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"støypau","last":"Støy","locid":"2a9c0934-98ca-4101-b829-b88d77f6487e","name":"Støy, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0003-0179-2487","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sebastian","key":"risipau","last":"Risi","locid":"a420ffb3-2ca6-4925-a25f-baaa5228fc15","name":"Risi, Sebastian","orcid":"0000-0003-3607-8400","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"eisbn":["9780262339360"],"pages":"692-699","publisher":"MIT Press","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the Artificial Life Conference 2016"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85087106895"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-32dfa560-6838-4b38-8c1f-ccd0a0fcb432"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Generating Artificial Plant Morphologies for Function and Aesthetics through Evolving L-Systems","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382007","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
